40. p − 1 = k
    algebra, from D1

41. 2^k·m = 2^(p − 1)·m
    substitute p − 1 = k (line 40)

42. 2^(p − 1)·m = 2^(p − 1)·(2^p − 1)
    substitute m = 2^p − 1 (line 34)

43. n = 2^(p − 1)·(2^p − 1)
    calculation
      n = 2^k·m                       1
        = 2^(p − 1)·m                 41
        = 2^(p − 1)·(2^p − 1)         42

44. There is p ∈ ℕ with 2^p − 1 is prime and n = 2^(p − 1)·(2^p − 1).
